INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — QTIP trust marital deduction election
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Ferrante dies in 2026, leaving a $6 million trust that pays all income annually to his surviving spouse, Rivera. Upon Rivera''s death, the remaining trust assets pass to Ferrante''s children from a prior marriage. The executor makes a valid QTIP election under IRC Section 2056(b)(7). What is the estate tax effect of the QTIP election on Ferrante''s estate?', '["The trust is excluded from Ferrante''s gross estate entirely", "The trust qualifies for the marital deduction but is included in Rivera''s gross estate at her death", "The trust is taxable in Ferrante''s estate because it is a terminable interest", "The trust qualifies for the charitable deduction instead of the marital deduction"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2056(b)(7), a QTIP election allows the executor to claim a marital deduction for trust assets that provide the surviving spouse with all income for life, even though the remainder passes to others. The election defers taxation until the surviving spouse''s death under Section 2044. Choice C is incorrect because the QTIP election specifically overrides the terminable interest rule that would otherwise disqualify the trust.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Three-year rule for life insurance policy transfer
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Donaldson transferred ownership of a $750,000 life insurance policy to an irrevocable trust on January 10, 2024. Donaldson died on August 5, 2025. The trust was the policy beneficiary and received the $750,000 death benefit. Analyze the estate tax consequence of Donaldson''s transfer.', '["The $750,000 is excluded from Donaldson''s gross estate because ownership was transferred before death", "The $750,000 death benefit is included in Donaldson''s gross estate because the transfer occurred within three years of death", "Only the cash surrender value at the date of transfer is included in the gross estate", "The death benefit is split equally between Donaldson''s gross estate and the trust"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2035(a), any transfer of a life insurance policy within three years of the decedent''s death causes the full death benefit to be included in the gross estate. Because Donaldson transferred the policy less than three years before death, the entire $750,000 is included under Section 2042 as read with Section 2035. Choice A is incorrect because the three-year survival period had not elapsed when Donaldson died.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Evaluate GRAT vs outright gift for appreciated asset
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Castellano, age 65, owns closely held stock valued at $4 million with a basis of $200,000. She considers transferring the stock through either a two-year grantor retained annuity trust or an outright gift. Her estate planner projects 12% annual appreciation. Castellano wants to minimize transfer taxes while retaining some income stream. Evaluate which strategy better serves Castellano''s objectives.', '["A GRAT is preferable because appreciation above the Section 7520 rate passes transfer-tax-free to remainder beneficiaries while Castellano retains annuity payments", "An outright gift is preferable because it immediately removes all future appreciation from the gross estate", "A GRAT is preferable because the entire $4 million is excluded from the gross estate upon funding", "An outright gift is preferable because gift tax does not apply to transfers of closely held stock"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2702, a GRAT allows the grantor to retain an annuity for a specified term, and only the remainder interest (appreciation exceeding the Section 7520 hurdle rate) is a taxable gift. Because the stock is projected to appreciate at 12%, well above the 7520 rate, the excess growth passes to beneficiaries transfer-tax-free. Choice B is incorrect because an outright gift triggers an immediate taxable gift of the full $4 million fair market value without providing the retained income stream Castellano desires.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Skip person definition for GST
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Under IRC Chapter 13, what is a skip person for generation-skipping transfer tax purposes?', '["A person assigned to the same generation as the transferor", "A person one generation below the transferor", "A trust in which no non-skip person has an interest", "A person assigned to a generation two or more generations below the transferor"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 2613(a), a skip person is an individual assigned to a generation two or more levels below the transferor, such as a grandchild. Alternatively, a trust qualifies as a skip person if no non-skip person holds an interest. Choice B is incorrect because a person one generation below the transferor, such as a child, is a non-skip person and transfers to that person are not subject to GST tax.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Net gift calculation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Ortega makes a gift of property worth $500,000 to his daughter, Montoya, on March 3, 2026, on the condition that Montoya pays any resulting gift tax. The applicable gift tax rate is 40%, and Ortega has fully used his lifetime exemption. After the $19,000 annual exclusion, calculate the net gift and the gift tax Montoya must pay.', '["Taxable gift of $481,000; gift tax of $192,400", "Taxable gift of $343,571; gift tax of $137,429", "Taxable gift of $500,000; gift tax of $200,000", "Taxable gift of $288,600; gift tax of $192,400"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the net gift doctrine applied through IRC Section 2512, when the donee agrees to pay gift tax, the taxable gift is reduced by the tax obligation. The formula is ($500,000 minus $19,000) divided by 1.40 = $343,571, with gift tax of $137,429. Choice A is incorrect because it applies the 40% rate to the full $481,000 without reducing for the donee''s tax payment obligation, which overstates both the gift and the tax.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Present interest requirement for annual exclusion
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'What must a gift be in order to qualify for the annual gift tax exclusion under IRC Section 2503(b)?', '["A gift of a present interest giving the donee an immediate right to use or enjoy the property", "A gift that does not exceed $19,000 in total value", "A gift made to a family member as defined under Section 267", "A gift of either a present or future interest in property"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2503(b), the annual gift tax exclusion applies only to gifts of present interests, meaning the donee must have an unrestricted right to the immediate use, possession, or enjoyment of the property. Future interests do not qualify regardless of value. Choice D is incorrect because future interests are specifically excluded from the annual exclusion under Section 2503(b).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Joint tenancy with non-spouse gross estate inclusion
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Palmer and Blake, who are siblings, purchased a vacation property as joint tenants with right of survivorship in 2018 for $400,000. Palmer contributed $300,000 and Blake contributed $100,000. Palmer dies in 2026 when the property is worth $600,000. What amount is included in Palmer''s gross estate?', '["$300,000, reflecting Palmer''s original contribution amount", "$200,000, reflecting Palmer''s share of post-purchase appreciation", "$450,000, reflecting the proportion of Palmer''s contribution to total cost", "$600,000, reflecting the full fair market value of the property"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2040(a), for joint tenants who are not spouses, the gross estate includes the portion of the property''s current value proportional to the decedent''s contribution. Palmer contributed $300,000 of $400,000 (75%), so 75% of the $600,000 date-of-death value, or $450,000, is included. Choice A incorrectly uses the original dollar contribution rather than the proportionate share of current fair market value.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Special use valuation election analysis
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Nakamura dies in 2026 owning a 200-acre farm valued at $3,000,000 for commercial development (highest and best use). The farm''s agricultural use value is $1,200,000, and the farm comprises 60% of the adjusted gross estate. The executor considers electing special use valuation. Analyze how the Section 2032A election affects the estate''s tax computation.', '["The election reduces the gross estate by $1,800,000 with no conditions on future use of the property", "The election defers estate tax on $1,800,000 until the property is sold or transferred to a nonfamily member", "The election permits a maximum reduction of $1,390,000 from the gross estate, and recapture applies if qualified use ceases within 10 years", "The election converts the farm to a nontaxable asset excluded entirely from the gross estate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2032A, the special use valuation election allows qualifying real property to be valued at its current use rather than highest and best use, subject to an indexed statutory cap ($1,390,000 for recent years). Although the actual difference is $1,800,000, the cap limits the reduction. Recapture tax under Section 2032A(c) applies if the qualified use ceases within 10 years. Choice A is incorrect because it ignores both the statutory cap on the reduction amount and the recapture requirement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Evaluate Section 6166 installment payment election
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Hoffman dies in 2026 owning a 45% interest in a closely held manufacturing business valued at $8 million, comprising 55% of her $14.5 million adjusted gross estate. The estate has liquid assets of $1.2 million but faces a $2.8 million estate tax liability. The executor evaluates whether to elect installment payments under IRC Section 6166. Assess the primary advantage and qualifying condition.', '["The election allows a full tax exemption for closely held business interests exceeding 35% of the adjusted gross estate", "The election defers estate tax attributable to the business interest, with interest-only payments for five years followed by up to ten annual installments, provided the business exceeds 35% of the adjusted gross estate", "The election converts the estate tax into a capital gains tax on the business interest payable over 15 years", "The election suspends all estate tax liability until the business is sold or transferred outside the family"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 6166, the executor may elect to pay estate tax attributable to a closely held business interest in installments if the business exceeds 35% of the adjusted gross estate. The estate pays interest only for five years, then up to ten equal annual installments at favorable rates. Hoffman''s 55% business interest qualifies. Choice D is incorrect because the tax is not suspended — it must be paid on schedule, with acceleration upon disposition.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Complete vs incomplete gift analysis
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Lancaster transfers $500,000 to a revocable trust on July 1, 2026, naming her nephew Briggs as beneficiary. Lancaster retains the power to revoke the trust at any time. On October 15, 2026, Lancaster relinquishes the revocation power, making the trust irrevocable. Analyze when the gift is complete for gift tax purposes.', '["The gift is complete on October 15, 2026, when Lancaster relinquishes dominion and control by surrendering the power to revoke", "The gift is complete on July 1, 2026, when the trust is funded with $500,000", "The gift is complete ratably over the period from July 1 to October 15, 2026", "The gift is never complete because it was initially placed in a revocable trust"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Treasury Regulation Section 25.2511-2(c), a gift is not complete until the donor relinquishes dominion and control over the transferred property. Because Lancaster retained the power to revoke, no completed gift occurred on July 1. The gift became complete on October 15, 2026, when she surrendered the revocation power. Choice B is incorrect because the initial funding of a revocable trust is not a completed transfer for gift tax purposes.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Adjusted taxable gifts in estate tax computation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Waverly dies in 2026 with a gross estate of $12 million and allowable deductions of $2 million. During her lifetime, Waverly made $3 million in taxable gifts and paid $400,000 in gift tax. Determine the tentative tax base for computing Waverly''s estate tax.', '["$10,000,000", "$13,000,000", "$12,600,000", "$15,000,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2001(b), the tentative tax base equals the taxable estate plus adjusted taxable gifts. The taxable estate is $12,000,000 minus $2,000,000 = $10,000,000. Adding $3,000,000 in adjusted taxable gifts yields $13,000,000. Choice A is incorrect because it uses only the taxable estate without adding lifetime gifts. The $400,000 gift tax previously paid is a credit against the computed tax, not an adjustment to the base.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Valuation discount analysis for closely held business
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Fujimoto dies in 2026 owning a 30% interest in a closely held LLC with total net assets of $10 million. The estate''s appraiser applies a 25% combined lack-of-control and lack-of-marketability discount. The IRS challenges the discount as excessive. Analyze the estate''s basis for claiming the valuation discount.', '["The estate must report $3,000,000 because valuation discounts are not permitted for LLC interests", "The estate may report $2,500,000 by applying the discount to the entity''s total value before calculating the share", "The discount applies only to interests in S corporations, not limited liability companies", "The estate may report $2,250,000 because minority interests lack control and marketability, reducing value below pro-rata net assets"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 2031, estate assets are valued at fair market value, which for closely held interests may reflect discounts for lack of control and lack of marketability per Estate of Mandelbaum. Fujimoto''s 30% of $10 million is $3,000,000 pro-rata, reduced 25% to $2,250,000. Choice A is incorrect because the Code requires fair market value, not pro-rata net asset value, and discounts are well-established for minority interests in closely held entities.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Gift tax marital deduction for citizen spouse
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'On February 14, 2026, Sinclair gives his wife, Ellison, a diamond necklace valued at $85,000 and a painting valued at $42,000. Ellison is a U.S. citizen. Determine the amount of Sinclair''s taxable gifts for 2026 from these transfers.', '["$0, because both gifts qualify for the unlimited marital deduction", "$108,000, reduced only by the $19,000 annual exclusion", "$127,000, representing the total value of both gifts", "$66,000, after applying two separate annual exclusions"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2523, an unlimited marital deduction is available for gifts to a spouse who is a U.S. citizen. Both the necklace and the painting qualify, reducing the taxable gift to $0 regardless of value. No annual exclusion analysis is necessary because the marital deduction eliminates the entire gift. Choice B is incorrect because it ignores the unlimited marital deduction available for transfers between citizen spouses.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Below-market loan imputed gift under Section 7872
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'On January 1, 2026, Thornberry lends $200,000 to her adult son, Keating, at 0% interest. The applicable federal rate for the loan term is 5.2%. No exceptions under IRC Section 7872 apply. Calculate the imputed gift for 2026.', '["$0, because family loans are exempt from imputed interest rules", "$19,000, limited to the annual gift tax exclusion amount", "$10,400, representing the foregone interest at the applicable federal rate", "$200,000, representing the full principal amount of the loan"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 7872, a below-market loan between family members results in an imputed gift equal to the foregone interest — the difference between interest at the applicable federal rate and the actual interest charged. Here, $200,000 times 5.2% equals $10,400 of foregone interest treated as a gift from Thornberry to Keating. Choice A is incorrect because Section 7872 specifically applies to gift loans between family members.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — General power of appointment definition
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Under IRC Section 2041, what is a general power of appointment?', '["A power to distribute trust assets only to the holder''s descendants", "A power exercisable in favor of the holder, the holder''s estate, the holder''s creditors, or creditors of the holder''s estate", "A power limited to an ascertainable standard relating to health, education, support, or maintenance", "A power exercisable only with the consent of all trust beneficiaries"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2041(b)(1), a general power of appointment is one exercisable in favor of the holder, the holder''s estate, the holder''s creditors, or creditors of the holder''s estate. Property subject to a general power is included in the holder''s gross estate. Choice C is incorrect because it describes a limited power subject to an ascertainable standard, which is excluded from the definition of a general power under Section 2041(b)(1)(A).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Taxable termination under GST
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Pearson established an irrevocable trust in 2015, naming her daughter Whitaker as income beneficiary and granddaughter Yates as remainder beneficiary. Pearson allocated her full GST exemption to other transfers. Whitaker dies in 2026, causing the trust to terminate and distribute to Yates. Analyze the generation-skipping transfer tax consequence.', '["No GST tax applies because the trust distributed to a lineal descendant of the transferor", "The termination is subject to income tax only, not generation-skipping transfer tax", "The distribution to Yates is a taxable termination subject to GST tax because no exemption was allocated to this trust", "The trust termination is exempt because it occurred more than 10 years after the trust was created"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2612(a), a taxable termination occurs when an interest in a trust terminates and thereafter only skip persons have interests. When Whitaker (non-skip person) dies, the trust distributes to Yates (skip person/grandchild), triggering a taxable termination. Because Pearson allocated her GST exemption elsewhere, this trust has an inclusion ratio of 1, making the full distribution taxable. Choice A is incorrect because lineal descent does not exempt a transfer from GST tax.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Evaluate QPRT strategy and mortality risk
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Marchetti, age 70, owns a vacation home valued at $2,500,000 and wants to transfer it to her children while minimizing transfer taxes. Her estate planner recommends a qualified personal residence trust with a 12-year retained interest term. Marchetti is concerned about the risk of dying during the trust term. Evaluate the tax consequences if Marchetti survives versus dies during the term.', '["If she survives, the home passes free of additional transfer tax; if she dies during the term, only the original gift tax value is included", "If she survives, the home is included in her estate at appreciated value; dying during the term voids the QPRT entirely", "If she dies during the term, the home passes tax-free to her children with no estate inclusion", "If she survives, the home passes transfer-tax-free at its appreciated value; if she dies during the term, the full date-of-death value is included under Section 2036"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 2702, a QPRT allows the grantor to transfer a personal residence at a reduced gift tax value by retaining a term interest. If Marchetti survives the 12-year term, the home passes to her children free of additional estate or gift tax regardless of appreciation. However, under Section 2036, if she dies during the retained interest term, the entire date-of-death value is included in her gross estate. Choice A is incorrect because the full current value, not the original gift value, is included upon death during the term.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — Three-year gross-up rule for gift tax paid
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Barlow paid $400,000 in gift tax on a $2 million taxable gift made in January 2024. Barlow dies in December 2025. Under the gross-up rule, what amount is added to Barlow''s gross estate?', '["$400,000, the gift tax paid on the gift made within three years of death", "$2,000,000, the full value of the taxable gift", "$2,400,000, the combined gift and gift tax amounts", "$0, because completed gifts are never added back to the gross estate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2035(b), gift tax paid on transfers made within three years of death is added back to the decedent''s gross estate (the gross-up rule). Because Barlow paid $400,000 in gift tax on a 2024 gift and died in 2025, the $400,000 is included. Choice B is incorrect because the gift itself is not brought back into the gross estate — only the gift tax paid is included, preventing depletion of the estate through deathbed transfers.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Future interest exclusion from annual exclusion
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Which type of gift does not qualify for the annual gift tax exclusion under IRC Section 2503(b)?', '["A gift of cash deposited into the donee''s bank account", "A gift of publicly traded stock transferred to the donee", "A gift of a present interest in a trust with immediate withdrawal rights", "A gift of a remainder interest in a trust payable upon the grantor''s death"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 2503(b), the annual exclusion applies only to gifts of present interests — those giving the donee an immediate right to use, possess, or enjoy the property. A remainder interest (choice D) is a future interest because the donee cannot access it until a future event, so it does not qualify. Unlike choices A, B, and C, which all represent present interests with immediate donee access, a remainder interest defers enjoyment to a future date.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Credit for tax on prior transfers (Section 2013)
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Reynolds inherited $800,000 from her mother in 2024, and her mother''s estate paid $180,000 in estate tax attributable to that inheritance. Reynolds dies in 2026. The executor claims a credit for tax on prior transfers. Analyze how the timing of Reynolds''s death affects the Section 2013 credit.', '["The credit equals $0 because the inherited property was consumed before death", "The credit equals 100% of $180,000 because Reynolds died within two years of her mother''s death", "The credit is reduced to 80% because Reynolds died between two and four years after the prior transfer", "The credit equals 50% of $180,000 because the property was inherited rather than gifted"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2013, a credit is allowed for estate tax paid on property transferred to a decedent who dies within 10 years. The credit is 100% if the decedent dies within two years, decreasing by 20% for each additional two-year period. Reynolds died in 2026, within two years of her mother''s 2024 death, so the full $180,000 credit applies. Choice C is incorrect because the 80% reduction applies only if the decedent died between two and four years after the prior transfer, which is not the case here.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Community property basis step-up analysis
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Morales and his wife, Fuentes, live in a community property state. Morales dies in 2026 owning community property worth $6 million (his half: $3 million) and separate property worth $2 million. Fuentes''s basis in her half of the community property is $1 million. Analyze the estate inclusion and basis consequences for the community property.', '["Only Morales''s $3 million half is included in his gross estate, and only his half receives a stepped-up basis", "The full $6 million of community property is included in Morales''s gross estate and both halves receive a stepped-up basis", "Morales''s gross estate includes his $3 million half, but both halves receive a stepped-up basis under IRC Section 1014(b)(6)", "The full $6 million is excluded because community property passes automatically to the surviving spouse"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2040, only Morales''s one-half interest ($3 million) in community property is included in his gross estate. However, under Section 1014(b)(6), both halves of community property receive a stepped-up basis to fair market value at the decedent''s death, benefiting Fuentes as well. Choice B incorrectly includes the full $6 million in the gross estate — only the decedent''s half is included for community property.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Incidents of ownership in life insurance
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Under IRC Section 2042, what are incidents of ownership in a life insurance policy?', '["The premium payments made by the policy owner during the insured''s lifetime", "Rights held by the decedent such as the power to change beneficiaries, borrow against the policy, or cancel it", "The cash surrender value of the policy at the date of the insured''s death", "The face amount of the death benefit payable to the named beneficiaries"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 2042(2), incidents of ownership include any economic rights the decedent held over a life insurance policy, such as the power to change beneficiaries, surrender or cancel the policy, assign the policy, or borrow against its cash value. If the decedent held any incidents at death, the full death benefit is included in the gross estate. Choice C is incorrect because cash surrender value is a policy attribute rather than a right of ownership that triggers estate inclusion under Section 2042.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Evaluate charitable remainder unitrust strategy
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Bergstrom, age 68, holds appreciated stock worth $3 million with a basis of $300,000. She wants income for 20 years and to benefit her alma mater with the remainder. Her CPA recommends a charitable remainder unitrust paying 5% annually. Bergstrom asks how this compares to selling the stock outright and donating proceeds at death. Evaluate the CRUT''s tax advantages.', '["The CRUT provides an immediate income tax deduction for the remainder interest, avoids capital gains on the contributed stock, and removes the asset from the gross estate", "The CRUT provides no income tax benefits but eliminates estate tax on the contributed property at death", "The CRUT and sell-and-donate approach produce identical tax results because charitable deductions offset gains in both cases", "The CRUT is disqualified because the 5% payout rate exceeds the minimum required by Section 664"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 664, a charitable remainder unitrust allows the donor an immediate income tax deduction for the present value of the charitable remainder interest, avoids recognizing capital gains on the contributed appreciated property, and removes the asset from the donor''s estate. Selling outright would trigger immediate capital gains tax on $2,700,000 of gain. Choice D is incorrect because the minimum payout rate under Section 664(d)(2) is 5%, so a 5% rate qualifies.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — State death tax deduction
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'What does IRC Section 2058 allow as a deduction from the federal gross estate?', '["Funeral and burial expenses of the decedent", "Debts owed by the decedent at the time of death", "Administration expenses incurred by the executor", "State death taxes actually paid by the estate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 2058, estates may deduct state death taxes (estate or inheritance taxes) actually paid to any state when computing the federal taxable estate. This deduction replaced the former state death tax credit under Section 2011. Choice A is incorrect because funeral expenses, along with debts and administration costs, are deductible under Section 2053 rather than Section 2058.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Taxable distribution definition for GST
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Under IRC Section 2612(b), what is a taxable distribution for generation-skipping transfer tax purposes?', '["Any distribution from a trust to a non-skip person", "A distribution that terminates the trust entirely", "A distribution from a trust to a skip person that is not a taxable termination or direct skip", "Any distribution from a trust exceeding the annual gift tax exclusion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 2612(b), a taxable distribution is any distribution from a trust to a skip person (an individual two or more generations below the transferor) that is not a taxable termination or a direct skip. The distributee is liable for the GST tax. Choice A is incorrect because distributions to non-skip persons are not subject to GST tax at all.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Unified transfer tax system
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'What does the unified transfer tax system under IRC Sections 2001 and 2501 combine?', '["The estate tax and the gift tax into a single cumulative system with a shared rate schedule and exemption", "The estate tax and the income tax for decedents into one return", "The gift tax and the generation-skipping transfer tax into one levy", "The estate tax and state inheritance taxes into a single federal framework"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Sections 2001 and 2501, the unified transfer tax system combines the estate tax and gift tax into a single cumulative framework with a shared progressive rate schedule and a unified applicable exclusion amount. Lifetime taxable gifts reduce the exemption available at death. Choice C is incorrect because the generation-skipping transfer tax under Chapter 13 operates as a separate supplemental tax, not part of the unified estate-gift system.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Part-gift, part-sale basis calculation
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Calderon sells property with a fair market value of $180,000 and an adjusted basis of $60,000 to his daughter, Reeves, for $100,000 on April 10, 2026. The transaction is treated as a part-gift, part-sale. Determine Reeves''s basis in the property.', '["$60,000, the donor''s adjusted basis carried over", "$100,000, the greater of the amount paid or the donor''s adjusted basis", "$180,000, the fair market value at the time of transfer", "$120,000, the cost of $100,000 adjusted for the gift element"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Treasury Regulation Section 1.1015-4, the donee''s basis in a part-gift, part-sale is the greater of the amount paid or the donor''s adjusted basis. Reeves paid $100,000, which exceeds Calderon''s adjusted basis of $60,000, so Reeves''s basis is $100,000. Choice A is incorrect because the carryover basis rule under Section 1015 is superseded by the greater-of rule when the donee provides consideration exceeding the donor''s basis.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: hard / L3 — Section 2036 retained interest inclusion analysis
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'In 2020, Hargrove transferred $2 million in assets to an irrevocable trust for her children but retained the right to receive 5% of the trust income annually. Hargrove dies in 2026, when the trust assets are valued at $3,200,000. The IRS asserts the trust assets are fully includable in Hargrove''s gross estate. Analyze whether the IRS position is correct.', '["The IRS is incorrect because the transfer was completed more than three years before death", "The IRS is incorrect because only the present value of the retained 5% income interest should be included", "The IRS is partially correct — only the original $2 million transfer amount is includable", "The IRS is correct because Section 2036(a)(1) includes the full date-of-death value when the decedent retained the right to income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 2036(a)(1), if the decedent transferred property but retained the right to income for life, the full date-of-death value of the transferred property is included in the gross estate. Hargrove''s retained 5% income right triggers inclusion of the entire $3,200,000. Choice A is incorrect because the three-year rule under Section 2035 is irrelevant when Section 2036 applies — the retained income interest, not the timing of the transfer, triggers full inclusion.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — GST exemption allocation and inclusion ratio
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Patterson creates an irrevocable trust in 2026 with a $5 million contribution for the benefit of her grandchildren. Patterson timely allocates $5 million of her GST exemption to the trust. Determine the inclusion ratio and the GST tax consequence of future distributions.', '["Inclusion ratio of 0; all future distributions to grandchildren are exempt from GST tax", "Inclusion ratio of 1; all distributions are subject to GST tax at 40%", "Inclusion ratio of 0.50; half of each distribution is subject to GST tax", "The inclusion ratio cannot be determined until the trust terminates and distributes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 2642(a), the inclusion ratio equals 1 minus the applicable fraction, where the fraction is the GST exemption allocated divided by the trust value. Patterson allocated $5 million to a $5 million trust, producing a fraction of 1 and an inclusion ratio of 0. All future distributions are fully exempt from GST tax. Choice B is incorrect because an inclusion ratio of 1 results only when no GST exemption is allocated to the trust, which is not the case here.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Evaluate ILIT funding strategy for estate liquidity
(3, 'Estate and Gift Tax', 'Vasquez, age 55, has a $20 million estate and wants to provide $5 million in liquid funds for estate tax payment without increasing his taxable estate. His estate planner proposes an irrevocable life insurance trust to purchase a $5 million policy. Vasquez would make annual gifts to the trust to cover premiums. Evaluate the critical requirement for excluding the death benefit from Vasquez''s gross estate.', '["Vasquez must name the trust as both owner and beneficiary, and premiums must be paid from trust funds held for more than one year", "Vasquez must purchase the policy personally and transfer it to the ILIT within 60 days of issuance to qualify", "Vasquez must retain no incidents of ownership, and the trust must be owner and beneficiary from inception or for more than three years before death", "Vasquez must ensure the trust holds diversified assets beyond the insurance policy to avoid disqualification"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Sections 2042 and 2035, the death benefit is excluded from the insured''s gross estate only if the insured holds no incidents of ownership in the policy. If the trust is owner and beneficiary from inception, no three-year rule applies. If the policy is transferred to the trust, the insured must survive three years under Section 2035. Choice B is incorrect because transferring a personally owned policy triggers the three-year inclusion rule with no 60-day safe harbor.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
